{"verdict":"changes","notes":"1. releaseAdmission drops and removes its flock before reading the claim, then deletes whatever SHA is current. A successor can CAS in a new claim between those operations; the stale release will read the successor's SHA and delete its claim, allowing a third participant to start while the successor is still working. The release must be tied to the holder's own observed claim, not just the current ref.\n2. For a hard kill in one checkout, the later process uses the same lock path as the abandoned claim. claimAdmission takes that flock before reading the claim, and lockHeld(holder.Lock) then observes the caller's own flock as held, so it refuses and leaves the durable claim in place forever. The crash test only covers a different checkout and misses this required recovery case.\n3. A daemon and `forest run` in the same checkout contend on the same lock path. holdLock returns before the claim is read, so actOnSubject returns a generic lock error/code 1 rather than errAdmissionHeld/the named admission refusal required by the command behavior.\n4. Claims from a foreign host are unconditionally refused even when the holder has crashed, and only the owning host can reclaim them. If that host never returns, the claim is permanent; this violates the requirement that a crashed participant must not hold admission permanently without a timeout-based lease.","reviewer":"verifier","model":"openrouter/openai/gpt-5.6-luna","def_sha":"8531e5c611fd2ed2","run_id":"20260808T105118Z-branch-forest/192-exclude-concurrent-work-on-one-s","time":"2026-08-08T10:53:40Z"}
